  "body": "```\n2:07:37 <•isaacs> Domenic_: i think having binary/string streams as a special case of object streams is not terrible\n12:08:39 <•isaacs> it loses high water marks and buffering, which is a nice feature for some cases.\n12:08:47 <•isaacs> but it's also a feature that's kinda hard to get right\n12:08:55 <Domenic_> i don't htink it necessarily does\n12:08:55 <•isaacs> especially in a general way\n12:09:04 <Domenic_> what if you used the .length property of the objects, or defaulted to 1\n12:09:19 <•isaacs> Domenic_: yeah... meh.\n12:09:23 <•isaacs> Domenic_: that seems weird\n12:09:37 <•isaacs> Domenic_: so an array is counted as thenumber of items, but a giant-ass object is counted as one thing?\n12:09:38 <Domenic_> seems nice, works for strings, arraybuffers, ...\n12:09:45 <•isaacs> it's a wart.\n12:09:50 <Domenic_> right, arrays are a bit of a strange case\n12:09:53 <•isaacs> warts always seem nice :)\n12:09:58 <•isaacs> until you have to implement and use them :)\n12:10:34 <Domenic_> i mean buffering is kind of crucial, no?\n12:12:22 <•isaacs> Domenic_: well, it must buffer if data is being put in, and not taken out\n12:12:47 <•isaacs> Domenic_: but, the \"buffer\" could just be \"I have >0 things already\"\n12:14:02 <•isaacs> Domenic_: for purposes of backpressure\n12:14:53 <Domenic_> isaacs: in-ter-est-ing...\n12:15:29 <•isaacs> Domenic_: you could conceivably build high water buffering stuff on top of that, i suppose\n```\n",
